# Migrating `BackgroundProcessor` to .NET 8

## Key Breaking Changes

1. **`Thread.Abort()` throws `PlatformNotSupportedException`** — removed entirely in .NET 5+
2. **`ThreadAbortException` will never be raised** — catch blocks for it are dead code
3. **`Thread.ResetAbort()` also throws `PlatformNotSupportedException`**

## Why This Pattern Existed

In .NET Framework, `Thread.Abort()` was the "nuclear option" for stopping blocked threads. It injected a `ThreadAbortException` at (almost) any point, which auto-rethrew unless `ResetAbort()` was called. This was always dangerous (corrupted state, locks held, etc.) but was the only way to interrupt certain blocking calls.

## Migrated Code (.NET 8)

```csharp
public class BackgroundProcessor : IAsyncDisposable
{
    private Task? _workerTask;
    private CancellationTokenSource? _cts;

    public void Start()
    {
        _cts = new CancellationTokenSource();
        _workerTask = Task.Run(() => ProcessLoopAsync(_cts.Token));
    }

    private async Task ProcessLoopAsync(CancellationToken token)
    {
        while (!token.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            try
            {
                // Pass the token so blocking calls become cancellable
                var item = await GetNextItemAsync(token);
                await ProcessItemAsync(item, token);
            }
            catch (OperationCanceledException) when (token.IsCancellationRequested)
            {
                // Graceful shutdown requested — replaces ThreadAbortException
                RollbackCurrentTransaction();
                break;
            }
            finally
            {
                FlushBuffers();
            }
        }
    }

    public async ValueTask Stop(TimeSpan? timeout = null)
    {
        if (_cts is null || _workerTask is null)
            return;

        timeout ??= TimeSpan.FromSeconds(5);

        // Signal cancellation (replaces _running = false + Thread.Abort)
        await _cts.CancelAsync();

        // Wait with a timeout instead of Thread.Sleep + Abort
        if (_workerTask != await Task.WhenAny(_workerTask, Task.Delay(timeout.Value)))
        {
            // Worker didn't stop in time — log a warning.
            // There is NO equivalent to Thread.Abort. You must design
            // all blocking operations to accept CancellationToken.
        }
    }

    public async ValueTask DisposeAsync()
    {
        await Stop();
        _cts?.Dispose();
    }
}
```

## Common Pitfalls

| Pitfall | Guidance |
|---------|----------|
| **Blocking call ignores token** | If `GetNextItem()` blocks on a `ManualResetEvent` or synchronous I/O, it will never observe cancellation. Migrate to async APIs (`Channel<T>.ReadAsync`, `SemaphoreSlim.WaitAsync`, etc.). |
| **Fire-and-forget `Task.Run`** | Always store the `Task` and observe exceptions. Unobserved exceptions crash the process in some hosting models. |
| **Replacing `volatile bool` with token** | `CancellationToken.IsCancellationRequested` is already thread-safe. No need for a separate `_running` flag. |
| **Assuming cancellation = immediate stop** | `OperationCanceledException` only fires at *cooperative* cancellation points. CPU-bound loops need periodic `token.ThrowIfCancellationRequested()`. |
| **Dispose race conditions** | Use `CancellationTokenSource.CancelAsync()` (.NET 8+) to avoid invoking callbacks on the calling thread. |

## If You Can't Make Blocking Code Async

As a last resort for truly uncooperative legacy code:

```csharp
// Dedicate a thread (not a thread-pool thread) so it can be abandoned
_workerTask = Task.Factory.StartNew(
    () => ProcessLoop(_cts.Token),
    _cts.Token,
    TaskCreationOptions.LongRunning,  // gets its own thread
    TaskScheduler.Default);
```

But **you cannot forcibly terminate it**. The only safe answer in .NET 8 is to make all blocking operations cancellation-aware. If external code truly cannot be interrupted, isolate it in a **separate process** and kill the process.
